    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelFunction">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#DeviceManagementFunctionCategory"/>
        <core:hasCommand rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelRetrievalCommand"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Function kind to manage the supported datamodel of a device.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Supported datamodel function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelParameterName -->


    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelParameterName">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://saref.etsi.org/core/Property"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Name of a supported datamodel parameter.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Supported datamodel parameter name</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelParameterProperty -->


    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelParameterProperty">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://saref.etsi.org/core/Property"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Property kind that represents the properties of a Supported Datamodel Parameter.

These parameters represent the capabilities of the Device in a format specific to the datamodel implemented by the Device, and exposed by the Client.

It should not be confused with the Datamodel Parameter Instance, which represents the actionable capabilities of the Device.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Supported datamodel parameter property</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelParameterType -->


    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelParameterType">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://saref.etsi.org/core/Property"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Type of a supported datamodel parameter. 

For instance, this type can represent that the supported datamodel parameter is a reference to an executable resource. The corresponding type would be &apos;command&apos; in the CWMP standard protocol, and its TR-181 technical report.

This type can represent a reference to a measurement process on the device that retrieves a value. In that case, the supported datamodel parameter also describes the unit of that value.

The type &apos;object&apos; corresponds to a supported datamodel parameter that contains other supported datamodel parameters, creating a hierarchical structure.

A final example would be a &apos;table&apos;, i.e., the corresponding datamodel parameter is a list of instances of the same object.

For more information, we refer the reader to the specification documents of DM protocol standards such as TR-181 from the BroadBand Forum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Supported datamodel parameter type</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelRetrievalCommand -->


    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelRetrievalCommand">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/dmont/ontology#SupportedDatamodelCommandCategory"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Command to retrieve a supported datamodel parameter, which corresponds to the information required to access a datamodel parameter instance and interpret its value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Supported datamodel retrieval command</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


